{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SVj1TXFcT66eVEJTsb2xgR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Checklist for `true-bdd scen check [scenario-id...]`.\n2\t#\n3\t# One cell per (scenario, prompt): each prompt reads ONE registry entry\n4\t# and rules on it alone. scen check is the verifier: a bare run exits 1\n5\t# when any scenario fails a prompt, so it gates a change, and every\n6\t# prompt carries an `F:`, so `--fix` walks the whole checklist.\n7\t#\n8\t# term_closure holds a scenario's words to the documents that define\n9\t# them. Its six `D:` prompts are answered by the engine's own checks,\n10\t# with no AI turn, before any `Q:`; a scenario failing one skips its\n11\t# `Q:` prompts until it is fixed. Their fixes write the product or the\n12\t# architecture document, never the scenario. Its two `Q:` prompts ask a\n13\t# model what no check can settle: whether a phrase's meaning says\n14\t# something, and whether the scenario uses the phrase in that meaning.\n15\t# The five phrase/term checks pass a scenario no Story claims, or a host\n16\t# whose product document declares no catalogue, as \"not checked\". The\n17\t# public-interface check holds every Scenario to its service declaration.\n18\t#\n19\t# scenario_quality holds the eight questions of the `us-refine`\n20\t# acceptance-criteria checklist that still mean something applied to a\n21\t# single scenario; its fixes rewrite the scenario's own registry entry.\n22\t# The four it dropped threshold over a set (AC count 3-7, at least one\n23\t# happy path, at least two error scenarios, at least 80% convertible).\n24\t\n25\tversion: \"1.0\"\n26\tlast_updated: \"2026-10-21\"\n27\tsections:\n28\t    - id: scenario_quality\n29\t      name: \"Scenario Quality\"\n30\t      description: \"Every registry scenario must read as one testable behaviour, in the product's vocabulary.\"\n31\t      validation_prompts:\n32\t        - Q: |\n33\t            Decide whether this scenario has a binary pass/fail outcome —\n34\t            whether a reviewer can decide pass or fail without subjective\n35\t            judgement, with no ambiguity about what counts as correct.\n36\t\n37\t            Pass when the outcome is binary.\n38\t            Fail when it is subjective.\n39\t\n40\t            Context: emit one line.\n41\t              - \"<scenario id>: binary\"\n42\t              - \"<scenario id>: subjective — '<reason>'\"\n43\t          rationale: \"A scenario a reviewer must interpret is a scenario two runs can disagree about while both being right.\"\n44\t          F: \"Rewrite the subjective outcome in this Scenario's registry entry, in its description and the step that asserts it, as one a reviewer decides without judgement, asking what counts as correct first. Keep the behaviour.\"\n45\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"15ceff29-9735-4a7e-8933-0282e52a9b90","uuid":"e397af86-8bbe-4954-bfac-d34e415bc73e","timestamp":"2026-10-30T14:52:21.787Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"{{CWD}}/true-bdd/checklists/scen-check.yaml","content":"# Checklist for `true-bdd scen check [scenario-id...]`.\n#\n# One cell per (scenario, prompt): each prompt reads ONE registry entry\n# and rules on it alone. scen check is the verifier: a bare run exits 1\n# when any scenario fails a prompt, so it gates a change, and every\n# prompt carries an `F:`, so `--fix` walks the whole checklist.\n#\n# term_closure holds a scenario's words to the documents that define\n# them. Its six `D:` prompts are answered by the engine's own checks,\n# with no AI turn, before any `Q:`; a scenario failing one skips its\n# `Q:` prompts until it is fixed. Their fixes write the product or the\n# architecture document, never the scenario. Its two `Q:` prompts ask a\n# model what no check can settle: whether a phrase's meaning says\n# something, and whether the scenario uses the phrase in that meaning.\n# The five phrase/term checks pass a scenario no Story claims, or a host\n# whose product document declares no catalogue, as \"not checked\". The\n# public-interface check holds every Scenario to its service declaration.\n#\n# scenario_quality holds the eight questions of the `us-refine`\n# acceptance-criteria checklist that still mean something applied to a\n# single scenario; its fixes rewrite the scenario's own registry entry.\n# The four it dropped threshold over a set (AC count 3-7, at least one\n# happy path, at least two error scenarios, at least 80% convertible).\n\nversion: \"1.0\"\nlast_updated: \"2026-10-21\"\nsections:\n    - id: scenario_quality\n      name: \"Scenario Quality\"\n      description: \"Every registry scenario must read as one testable behaviour, in the product's vocabulary.\"\n      validation_prompts:\n        - Q: |\n            Decide whether this scenario has a binary pass/fail outcome —\n            whether a reviewer can decide pass or fail without subjective\n            judgement, with no ambiguity about what counts as correct.\n\n            Pass when the outcome is binary.\n            Fail when it is subjective.\n\n            Context: emit one line.\n              - \"<scenario id>: binary\"\n              - \"<scenario id>: subjective — '<reason>'\"\n          rationale: \"A scenario a reviewer must interpret is a scenario two runs can disagree about while both being right.\"\n          F: \"Rewrite the subjective outcome in this Scenario's registry entry, in its description and the step that asserts it, as one a reviewer decides without judgement, asking what counts as correct first. Keep the behaviour.\"\n","numLines":45,"startLine":1,"totalLines":45}}}
