new text, so the applier replaces rather than composes.\n259\t- Never edit a generated test, a step definition, or a document the fix\n260\t  template does not name.\n261\t\n262\t=== FILE_START: {{.ResultPath}} ===\n263\t# Fix Prompt for Scenario {{.SubjectID}}: {{.Subject.Description}}\n264\t\n265\t## Target\n266\tThe `{{.Subject.ID}}` entry of the `scenarios_yaml` document (the\n267\tscenario registry), or the catalogue entries of the `product` document\n268\tthe findings name.\n269\t\n270\t## Required Changes\n271\t### Change #N: [description]\n272\t**Document:** <scenarios_yaml | product>\n273\t**Field:** <description | merged_steps.<given|when|then> | the means of catalogue entry '<template>'>\n274\t**Current text:**\n275\t```\n276\t<the text as it stands>\n277\t```\n278\t**New text:**\n279\t```\n280\t<the replacement>\n281\t```\n282\t=== FILE_END: {{.ResultPath}} ===\n283\t\n284\t**If you need clarification first**, output questions with suggested\n285\tanswers: each question offers the options you would accept, the one you\n286\trecommend first.\n287\t\n288\t=== QUESTIONS_START ===\n289\tquestions:\n290\t  - id: q1\n291\t    question: \"<your question>\"\n292\t    context: \"<why you need this information>\"\n293\t    options:\n294\t      - \"<the option you recommend>\"\n295\t      - \"<another option>\"\n296\t=== QUESTIONS_END ===\n297\t\n298\t**Important:**\n299\t- Output EXACTLY ONE block (FILE_START/FILE_END or QUESTIONS_START/QUESTIONS_END)\n300\t- Never output both\n301\t\n302\t{{ if .Structured }}\n303\t**ANSWER CONTRACT FOR THIS RUN — this supersedes the FILE_START/FILE_END\n304\tand QUESTIONS_START/QUESTIONS_END instructions above.** Your final message\n305\tmust be a single JSON object with both keys present:\n306\t\n307\t- `fix_prompt`: the fix instructions, or `\"\"` when you need clarification.\n308\t- `questions`: an array of `{id, question, context, options}` objects, or\n309\t  `[]` when you are returning a fix prompt.\n310\t\n311\tExactly one of the two carries content. Do not emit any markers, and do not\n312\twrap the object in markdown fences. Reasoning before the object is ignored.\n313\t{{ end }}\n314\t{{ else -}}\n315\t# Generate Fix Prompt for a Registry Scenario\n316\t\n317\t## Reference Documentation\n318\t{{- range $key, $path := .DocPaths }}\n319\t- Read(`{{ $path }}`) — {{ $key }}\n320\t{{- end }}\n321\t\n322\t## Where the fix goes\n323\t\n324\t{{ if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"term-mappings\" -}}\n325\tInto the term documents, and nowhere else: here the architecture\n326\tdocument's word mapping, its `architecture.terms:` list. A term-mapping\n327\tfinding says a product word a Scenario relies on has no stated\n328\timplementation: nothing says what it is in the code, or how a step\n329\tdefinition sets it up or observes it. The answer is a mapping, written\n330\tonce, where every Scenario using the word reads it. The Scenario, the\n331\tgenerated test rendered from it, the step definitions and the product\n332\tdocument are never edited by this fix.\n333\t{{- else -}}\n334\tInto the term documents, and nowhere else: the product document's\n335\tstep-phrase catalogue and glossary. A scen-check finding says a phrase\n336\tor a word a Scenario relies on has no stated meaning. The answer is a\n337\tdefinition, written once, where every Scenario using the phrase reads\n338\tit. The Scenario, the generated test rendered from it and the step\n339\tdefinitions are never edited by a scen-check fix. The fix template below\n340\tsays which document, and which kind of entry, the failed check wants.\n341\t{{- end }}\n342\t\n343\t## Subject — Registry Scenario\n344\t\n345\t**Scenario ID:** {{.Subject.ID}}\n346\t**Description:** {{.Subject.Description}}\n347\t**Service:** {{.Subject.Service}}\n348\t**Generated test file:** {{.Subject.Path}}\n349\t\n350\t### Steps\n351\t{{.Subject.FormatSteps}}\n352\t### Lineage — user_stories[]\n353\t{{.Subject.FormatUserStories}}\n354\t## Validation Failure\n355\t\n356\tThe following check failed and needs to be fixed:\n357\t\n358\t### Failed Check: {{ .FailedCheck.SectionPath }}\n359\t\n360\t**Check:** {{ .FailedCheck.Question }}\n361\t**Why it matters:** {{ .FailedCheck.Rationale }}\n362\t**Actual:** {{ .FailedCheck.ActualAnswer }}\n363\t\n364\t**Findings** (one line per failing step or entry):\n365\t{{- range .FailedCheck.Context }}\n366\t- {{ . }}\n367\t{{- end }}\n368\t\n369\t**Fix template:**\n370\t{{ .FailedCheck.FixPrompt }}\n371\t\n372\t## The catalogue\n373\t\n374\tA catalogue entry has four fields:\n375\t\n376\t- `template`: the shape of a step, in the grammar below. It is the\n377\t  entry's identity: no two entries share one.\n378\t- `block`: `given`, `when` or `then`, the one block a step of this shape\n379\t  may sit in.\n380\t- `means`: what a step of this shape means, in product words a reader\n381\t  of the Scenario understands; it may name the template's slots.\n382\t- `terms`: the glossary terms that meaning relies on, each spelled\n383\t  exactly as the glossary spells it; the list may be empty.\n384\t\n385\tEvery step of a Scenario a Story claims must match exactly one template\n386\tin the whole catalogue, and that entry's block must be the step's block.\n387\tA step opening with `llm:` or `judge:` is run by a model and exempt.\n388\t\n389\tTemplate grammar, token by token:\n390\t\n391\t- literal text: itself, exactly, case and whitespace included.\n392\t- `\"{name}\"`: one or more characters other than `\"`; the slot sits\n393\t  between literal double quotes.\n394\t- `{name:number}`: digits, or the word `no` for zero.\n395\t- `{name:role}`: exactly one `roles[].name` of the product document.\n396\t- `{name:pattern}`: the rest of the step, verbatim; only as the\n397\t  template's last token, after a space.\n398\t- `[a|b]`: exactly one of the literal alternatives.\n399\t- `[s]`: `s`, or nothing.\n400\t- `\\{`, `\\}`, `\\[`, `\\]`, `\\\\`: the literal character.\n401\t\n402\tSlot names are lower-case letters and underscores, unique within a\n403\ttemplate. A template matches a step's whole text, never a part of it.\n404\t\n405\tA good entry phrases the step in the shape other Scenarios will reuse:\n406\tquoted runs and numbers become slots, the words that carry the meaning\n407\tstay literal. It must not also match a step another entry already\n408\tphrases, or that step becomes ambiguous.\n409\t{{- if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"declared-terms\" }}\n410\t\n411\t## The glossary\n412\t\n413\tThe product document's `glossary:` list defines the words catalogue\n414\tentries declare in their `terms`. A glossary entry has two fields:\n415\t\n416\t- `term`: the word, spelled exactly as the catalogue entries declaring\n417\t  it spell it, case and spacing included. A term is looked up by that\n418\t  spelling and nothing else.\n419\t- `means`: what the word is in this product and why it matters to a\n420\t  reader of a Scenario, in product words. It says what the word means,\n421\t  never how the code implements it.\n422\t\n423\tEvery term a catalogue entry declares, through a step that matches\n424\tthat entry, must be defined exactly once. A term defined twice has two\n425\tmeanings, so a reader can rely on neither.\n426\t\n427\tA good definition reads true in every Scenario whose phrases declare\n428\tthe term, not only in this one: read the `means` of each catalogue\n429\tentry declaring it before you write it.\n430\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"term-mappings\" }}\n431\t\n432\t## The word mapping\n433\t\n434\tThe architecture document's `architecture.terms:` list maps each\n435\tproduct word the catalogue entries declare in their `terms` onto the\n436\timplementation. A mapping has three fields:\n437\t\n438\t- `term`: the word, spelled exactly as the catalogue entries declaring\n439\t  it spell it, case and spacing included. A term is looked up by that\n440\t  spelling and nothing else.\n441\t- `technical`: what the word is in the implementation: the process,\n442\t  request, record, file or field that carries it, named in words.\n443\t- `observed_by`: how a step definition sets the word up or observes\n444\t  it, in words a reader can follow to write that definition: what it\n445\t  starts, sends or reads, and what it compares. It never names a code\n446\t  identifier, so the definition can be rebuilt from it.\n447\t\n448\tEvery term a catalogue entry declares, through a step that matches\n449\tthat entry, must be mapped exactly once. A term mapped twice is built\n450\ttwo ways, so no step definition can follow both.\n451\t\n452\tA good mapping holds for every Scenario whose phrases declare the\n453\tterm, not only for this one: read the `means` of each catalogue entry\n454\tdeclaring it, the term's glossary entry, and the mappings already in\n455\tthe architecture document before you write it, and say the same kind\n456\tof thing the existing mappings say.\n457\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"step-definitions\" }}\n458\t\n459\t## The step definitions\n460\t\n461\tThe suite's step definitions, in the `steps/` directory the findings\n462\tname, bind each step's text to code. Catalogue templates and step\n463\tdefinitions must correspond one to one over the steps of every Scenario\n464\ta Story claims in that suite: every step matching one template binds to\n465\tone and the same definition, and each such definition is reached by\n466\tthat template alone. A step no definition binds yet is not a finding.\n467\t\n468\tA fix here changes the catalogue only: split a template whose steps\n469\tbind several definitions into one entry per definition, or merge the\n470\ttemplates that reach one definition into one entry. The step\n471\tdefinitions are never edited by a scen-check fix; when no catalogue\n472\tchange can close a finding, say so in the fix prompt instead of\n473\tproposing one.\n474\t{{- end }}\n475\t\n476\t{{- if .UserAnswers }}\n477\t\n478\t## User Clarifications (from previous questions)\n479\t\n480\tThe user provided the following clarifications:\n481\t{{- range $id, $answer := .UserAnswers }}\n482\t{{- if ne $id \"_user_refinement\" }}\n483\t- **{{ $id }}**: {{ $answer }}\n484\t{{- end }}\n485\t{{- end }}\n486\t\n487\tUse these answers to generate a confident fix. Do not ask these\n488\tquestions again.\n489\t{{- end }}\n490\t\n491\t{{- if index .UserAnswers \"_user_refinement\" }}\n492\t\n493\t## REFINEMENT MODE — User Feedback (CRITICAL)\n494\t\n495\tThe user has reviewed your PREVIOUS fix prompt and is providing\n496\tfeedback to CORRECT IT:\n497\t\n498\t> {{ index .UserAnswers \"_user_refinement\" }}\n499\t\n500\t**CRITICAL INSTRUCTIONS FOR REFINEMENT:**\n501\t1. **DO NOT ask more questions**\n502\t2. **Address the specific issue** — Fix exactly what the user pointed out\n503\t3. **Keep everything else** — Preserve parts of your previous fix that\n504\t   weren't criticized\n505\t4. **Output a fix prompt** — You MUST output FILE_START/FILE_END,\n506\t   NEVER QUESTIONS_START/QUESTIONS_END\n507\t{{- end }}\n508\t\n509\t## Your Task\n510\t\n511\t{{- if index .UserAnswers \"_user_refinement\" }}\n512\t**REFINEMENT MODE**: Apply user feedback and regenerate the fix prompt.\n513\tDO NOT ask questions.\n514\t{{- else if .UserAnswers }}\n515\tUsing the user's clarifications above, generate a complete fix prompt.\n516\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"declared-terms\" }}\n517\tDecide whether the Scenario and the documents already say what each\n518\tterm the findings name means. When the fix template asks you to ask\n519\tfirst, or the meaning is not plain, ask; otherwise generate the fix.\n520\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"term-mappings\" }}\n521\tDecide whether the Scenario and the documents already say how each\n522\tterm the findings name is implemented and how a step definition\n523\tobserves it. When the fix template asks you to ask first, or that is\n524\tnot plain, ask; otherwise generate the fix.\n525\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"step-definitions\" }}\n526\tDecide whether the Scenario and the documents already say which phrases\n527\tmean the same thing and which differ. When the fix template asks you to\n528\task first, or that is not plain, ask; otherwise generate the fix.\n529\t{{- else }}\n530\tDecide whether the Scenario and the documents already say what the\n531\tfailing step means. When the fix template asks you to ask first, or the\n532\tmeaning is not plain, ask; otherwise generate the fix.\n533\t{{- end }}\n534\t\n535\t**If you can generate a confident fix**, output a fix prompt the applier\n536\tcan execute against the term document. The fix MUST:\n537\t{{- if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"declared-terms\" }}\n538\t- Add or change only the glossary entries the findings call for: for a\n539\t  term the glossary does not define, one new entry defining it; for a\n540\t  term it defines more than once, a single entry in place of them all.\n541\t- Leave the catalogue, and every glossary entry the findings do not\n542\t  name, exactly as they are.\n543\t- Give the exact YAML of each entry, so the applier copies it rather\n544\t  than composing it.\n545\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"term-mappings\" }}\n546\t- Add or change only the mappings the findings call for: for a term\n547\t  the architecture does not map, one new entry mapping it; for a term\n548\t  it maps more than once, a single entry in place of them all.\n549\t- Leave every other part of the architecture document, and the product\n550\t  document, exactly as they are.\n551\t- Give the exact YAML of each entry, so the applier copies it rather\n552\t  than composing it.\n553\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"step-definitions\" }}\n554\t- Change only the catalogue entries the findings name: split or merge\n555\t  them so each template binds to one step definition and each\n556\t  definition is reached by one template.\n557\t- Leave every step that matches exactly one template today matching\n558\t  exactly one template, and every other entry as it is.\n559\t- Give the exact YAML of each entry, its template single-quoted, so the\n560\t  applier copies it rather than composing it.\n561\t{{- else }}\n562\t- Add or change only the entries the findings call for: for a step that\n563\t  matches no template, one new entry phrasing it.\n564\t- Leave every other step of this Scenario, and every step the other\n565\t  entries phrase, matching exactly one template.\n566\t- Give the exact YAML of each entry, its template single-quoted, so the\n567\t  applier copies it rather than composing it.\n568\t{{- end }}\n569\t- Never edit the Scenario, a generated test, a step definition or\n570\t  another document.\n571\t\n572\t=== FILE_START: {{.ResultPath}} ===\n573\t# Fix Prompt for Scenario {{.SubjectID}}: {{.Subject.Description}}\n574\t{{- if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"declared-terms\" }}\n575\t\n576\t## Target\n577\tThe `product` document: its `glossary:` list.\n578\t\n579\t## Required Changes\n580\t### Change #N: [description]\n581\t**Document:** product\n582\t**Action:** Append this entry to the end of the `glossary:` list\n583\t(or: replace old_string with new_string, verbatim).\n584\t**YAML:**\n585\t```yaml\n586\t  - term: <the term, spelled exactly as the catalogue declares it>\n587\t    means: <what the word is in this product, and why it matters>\n588\t```\n589\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"term-mappings\" }}\n590\t\n591\t## Target\n592\tThe `architecture_yaml` document: its `architecture.terms:` list.\n593\t\n594\t## Required Changes\n595\t### Change #N: [description]\n596\t**Document:** architecture_yaml\n597\t**Action:** Append this entry to the end of the `architecture.terms:`\n598\tlist, creating the list under `architecture:` when there is none (or:\n599\treplace old_string with new_string, verbatim).\n600\t**YAML:**\n601\t```yaml\n602\t    - term: <the term, spelled exactly as the catalogue declares it>\n603\t      technical: <what the word is in the implementation>\n604\t      observed_by: <how a step definition sets it up or observes it>\n605\t```\n606\t{{- else }}\n607\t\n608\t## Target\n609\tThe `<key>` document: its `catalogue:` list (or its `glossary:` list).\n610\t\n611\t## Required Changes\n612\t### Change #N: [description]\n613\t**Document:** <key>\n614\t**Action:** Append this entry to the end of the `catalogue:` list\n615\t(or: replace old_string with new_string, verbatim).\n616\t**YAML:**\n617\t```yaml\n618\t  - template: '<template>'\n619\t    block: <given|when|then>\n620\t    means: <what a step of this shape means>\n621\t    terms: [<glossary term>, ...]\n622\t```\n623\t{{- end }}\n624\t=== FILE_END: {{.ResultPath}} ===\n625\t\n626\t**If you need clarification first**, output questions with suggested\n627\tanswers: each question offers the options you would accept, the one you\n628\trecommend first.\n629\t\n630\t=== QUESTIONS_START ===\n631\tquestions:\n632\t  - id: q1\n633\t    question: \"<your question>\"\n634\t    context: \"<why you need this information>\"\n635\t    options:\n636\t      - \"<the option you recommend>\"\n637\t      - \"<another option>\"\n638\t=== QUESTIONS_END ===\n639\t\n640\t**Important:**\n641\t- Output EXACTLY ONE block (FILE_START/FILE_END or QUESTIONS_START/QUESTIONS_END)\n642\t- Never output both\n643\t\n644\t{{ if .Structured }}\n645\t**ANSWER CONTRACT FOR THIS RUN — this supersedes the FILE_START/FILE_END\n646\tand QUESTIONS_START/QUESTIONS_END instructions above.** Your final message\n647\tmust be a single JSON object with both keys present:\n648\t\n649\t- `fix_prompt`: the fix instructions, or `\"\"` when you need clarification.\n650\t- `questions`: an array of `{id, question, context, options}` objects, or\n651\t  `[]` when you are returning a fix prompt.\n652\t\n653\tExactly one of the two carries content. Do not emit any markers, and do not\n654\twrap the object in markdown fences. Reasoning before the object is ignored.\n655\t{{ end }}\n656\t{{ end -}}\n657\t{{ end -}}\n658\t{{ end -}}\n659\t{{ end -}}\n660\t{{ end